<string id="he_pda_cook_ask"><text>Docent, is there anything worth putting in a PDA to help with cooking?</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_offer"><text>I can. There is a module that hooks a PDA up to the scientists' monitoring stations: throw your artifacts into an anomaly, and it will tell you where the batch is cooking, roughly how long to wait, and mark the spot on the map. As for the strength of the anomaly itself, it can only make a rough guess, the stations measure coarsely, so do not get your hopes up. It works only in a working, charged PDA, and it will cost you four thousand.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_accept"><text>Deal. Here is the PDA and the money.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_wait"><text>One moment. I will open it up and fit the module.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_done"><text>Done. Just remember: it will show you what is cooking, but what comes out is up to the Zone.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_missing_ask"><text>What do you need from me? Or is there something to settle first?</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_missing"><text>An ordinary PDA on you, working and charged, plus four thousand. If your device is with a technician for repair or charging, collect it first. And if it has burnt out, get it fixed, there is no point fitting anything into a dead box.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_installed"><text>Transmutation monitoring module installed.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_started"><text>Transmutation started: %s. Time to completion: %s.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_hours_lt1"><text>less than an hour</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_hours"><text>about %d h</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_spot"><text>Transmutation in progress. Time to completion: %s.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_reminder_title"><text>Transmutations in progress</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_reminder_line"><text>%s: %s left</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_a1"><text>The readings are sluggish: the anomaly seems one of the quieter ones, though the station may have undercounted.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_a2"><text>The PDA shows a flat, shallow background: somewhere between quiet and ordinary, if the station is to be believed.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_a3"><text>According to the monitoring data, the anomaly breathes evenly with no surges, but the accuracy is rough, as always.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_a4"><text>Judging by the graph, the anomaly is not a wild one: ordinary, maybe quieter, though the station could be wrong.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_a5"><text>The signal is even, with no peaks, which looks like a calm anomaly, but readings like these are not to be taken on faith.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_b1"><text>The readings jump about: the anomaly seems lively, a bit nastier than usual, though the station may be exaggerating.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_b2"><text>The PDA logs frequent surges: somewhere between ordinary and vicious, if the station is not lying.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_b3"><text>According to the monitoring data, the anomaly is restless and the background wanders, but the stations measure coarsely, so take it as a guess.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_b4"><text>Judging by the graph, the anomaly is livelier than usual, but by how much the station cannot really say.</text></string>
<string id="he_pda_cook_read_b5"><text>The curve on the screen is jittery and full of peaks: the anomaly seems to be in good form, but readings like these often mislead.</text></string>
